Neither are easy to define.

{za'i}'s problem is that it involves the "boundary" of events, which lojban
is not very good at expressing. (As I think I mentioned earlier, the "fuzzy
boundary" of events is really a big reason why reasoning about events in
lojban is difficult in general.) My intuition about {za'i} is that it is
much like a concrete {du'u}; if you "want something to be true" where its
truth is "definite", you want a {za'i} rather than an ordinary {nu}.

{zu'o} is inseparable from {rapli} but also seems to have some connotation
of {gasnu} and {zukte}.

mu'o mi'e la latro'a

